hey everyone this is lucy and today i'll  be talking about how i passed the aws  certified solutions after professional  exam in 2022. if you're new to this  channel my name is lucy and i make  videos all about tech career and life at  aws so if you're interested in seeing  more videos like this please make sure  you click on subscribe down below and  give this video a like so i passed this  exam a couple days ago and i was pretty  happy about it because this has been on  my to-do list for the longest time now  and it's more of a long-term sort of  goal because you really need a couple  months to prepare for it so i wanted to  share how i was able to pass this exam  by sharing my resources advice and also  my exam strategies and experience as  well let's just jump right into it so  this exam is three hours long with 75  multiple choice multi-select questions  and it was a pretty intense exam really  glad i was able to dedicate a couple  months leading up to the exam to really  spend some time doing some direct  preparation so speaking of direct  preparation what i used was two main  resources first of all i used video  courses then i used practice exams and i  went through this whole cycle of doing  video courses then doing some practices  exams to check up on my knowledge and  what gaps i need to feel then going back  to those video courses re-watching some  of the videos and kind of continuing on  the cycle so for the video courses there  are two that i would like to recommend  so the first resource is adrian cantor's  solutions architect professional  certification video course so this video  course has a bunch of theory videos and  also hands-on labs and it really goes  above and beyond in terms of the level  of detail that it provides it teaches  you not only to pass the exam but also  teaches you the skills and the knowledge  to become a good essay so this course  was pretty interesting i didn't end up  doing the whole course because it was  very very long um and i wanted to focus  more time on practice questions instead  but i managed to get through a good  chunk of this course did a lot of  hands-on lap exercises and found it  really helpful in making me feel  confident in learning some of the  architectural patterns that might come  up for the exam i switched over to  another video course which is neil  davis's digital cloud training course  for the solutions architect professional  exam so this course was a lot more  concise it taught me exactly what was  needed for the exam and i also had a  really good amount of hands-on exercises  as well so that i could follow along and  gain a deeper knowledge  about each of the services and each of  the different sort of patterns that i'll  see whether that be traditional  three-tier architectures or serverless  web applications so in terms of practice  exams there's a whole bunch available  for free that you can find but what i  really liked about using paid resources  is that they have more detailed  explanations so when you get a question  wrong it really just tells you why you  got it wrong and why the correct answer  is the correct one so that's something i  really appreciated and the practice  question set that i really liked and i  used was tutorial dojos practice exams  because they had really in-depth  explanations when it came to those  questions and by doing a bunch of those  i was able to get more familiar with the  the format of the questions  and after doing that i was getting  some friends recommending me another set  of practice exams so i bought those as  well and did those those were neil  davis's digital cloud training practice  exams and  what i found good about these were that  it actually reflected some of the real  exam questions so when it came to me  doing the real exam a lot of the  questions seemed kind of you know i kind  of feel like i've done it before because  they were i think quite similar to some  of the questions that neil has put in  his practice exams so doing both of  those set of exams from both tutorials  dojo and digital cloud training really  set me up well when it came to setting  my real exam now let's talk about my  advice for actually preparing for the  exam the resources are really good but  when it comes down to actually  dedicating time the thing that's most  important is building consistency and  momentum when it comes to your study  it's really hard when we're working  full-time or we have other commitments  to really dedicate time consistently to  our aws journey and it can be quite  tempting to just say okay on the  weekends i'm just going to block out 10  hours and just going to study non-stop  but what works best for me and what i  recommend doing is to dedicate one to  two hours on a weekday actually  preparing for it and if you have time on  the weekend you can do a couple of hours  of study then and if not you can just  take the weekend off because you've done  some work already on the weekdays so  that's something i'll recommend trying  to build up consistency  my second piece of advice is that  practical experience is always more  important than theoretical knowledge so  make sure that you do all the hands-on  exercises provided by the video courses  rather than writing notes all the time  trying to memorize every single line and  every single feature of aws services  it's so much more important for you to  recognize patterns in architectures and  that way it's better for long-term  retention as well and it's better for  you when you actually want to become a  solutions architect in the future and  honestly i think my day-to-day work  really helped me with the solutions  arctic professional exam because i was  for example building a demo of a 3d web  application and through that i gained so  much knowledge about how 3d web apps  work and when it came to preparing for  the exam i found it so much more easier  to just understand the question of what  it was really asking as opposed to  trying to remember what i had memorized  from months ago okay finally let's talk  about exam strategy on the day what can  you do during those three hours to  maximize your chances of passing the  exam  so first of all i recommend something  called reverse flagging so you know when  you have a question that you're not sure  about you're flagging it that way i  think it doesn't really work for me what  i did was  i flagged the ones i was sure i got the  answer to so if i'm certain that the  answer is correct well i'm 90 sure that  as is correct i'll flag it and that way  i feel like every time i flag a question  it builds up my confidence rather than  every time i flag it i'm unsure and it  just shows myself that oh i need to  check back on it later so  by the end of the exam i would have  flags let's say 80 of the questions and  i will look back at the unflagged ones  to go back and  you know review the answer and try and  read over the question again something  else that i found to be quite useful was  to close my eyes for 10 to 20 seconds  every 10 to 20 minutes it sounds like  it's a waste of time because you only  have three hours for 75 questions but it  really helps for you to sort of regain  focus and just prevent eye strain during  the during the exam as well because  three hours looking at a screen it's  pretty pretty tough so  that's something i would recommend and  finally make sure you don't study on the  day of the exam there's already so much  that you've gained in the months or  weeks leading up to the exam i think on  the day if you try and grab more  information it might just overwhelm you  when it comes to the actual exam and you  want to be as relaxed as possible before  the exam actually starts  alright so this brings us to the end of  this video if you found it useful please  give this video a like leave your  comments and questions down below and  i'll see you next time best of luck for  your exam and bye for now
 

